  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They couldn't convert early chances and never recovered from the third inning.",
    "what_this_means": "Miami scored three in the third and the Athletics never answered. They had runners in scoring position ten times and converted just twice, leaving ten on base across a scoreless fourth through sixth. Civale gave them a clean four-plus innings. That was enough. But the offense went silent when it mattered. Nine strikeouts and two runs. Joshua Kuroda-Grauer kept them breathing with two hits, but breathing is not the same as fighting back. The game was decided by the third inning and the rest was watching the deficit hold.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what happens when the window opens and nobody steps through. They don't lack the pieces to score early. They lacked the execution. Four consecutive runners left on base in the first two innings is not a slump. That's a shutdown loss disguised as a close game. Kade Morris gave up three runs in three innings, but that was cleanup work for a game already gone. The division gap grew to four and a half because they had their moment in inning one and squandered it before Miami found theirs in inning three. These margins don't stay still.",
